INT: And then how did things go as the war outbreak got closer, as far as the situation for the Jews in your town?

LD: We didn't feel so much when the war started. We saw the Ukraine populations preparing to celebrate something. We feel this. Everybody dressed nice, clean-- the Ukrainian people. We were surrounded with lots of Ukrainian people. And every-- each sharpening the teeth. No, we didn't feel that right away.

Now, people were scared, and they evacuated, and they left the town. And just wealthy people, they had-- there was-- they have opinion-- they used to have opinion the German will not touch them [INAUDIBLE] [? the ?] rich. And the German is killing only the workers, the poor, and the Communist.

And this kept them-- not all of them-- partly-- to stay and not to leave his own. And some of them was really difficult to make a decision, extreme decision. In a normal life, in his own home, and to leave it. And to go-- they didn't know where to go. And to grab something what they can only carry in the two hand and go by feet. It was not everybody what could make the decision.

So I would say, in my town, half of the people didn't left. They--

INT: Stayed.

LD: Yes.

INT: But half of the people--

LD: They killed them.

INT: --left?

LD: Half of the people left-- evacuated.

INT: Where did they go?

LD: Pardon me?

INT: The people-- the people that left-- where did they go?

LD: They gone to deep Russia-- gone to the station. The-- some of them took off in trains, and some of them by feet, and some of them rent horses.

And what way [INAUDIBLE] they gone different way. To back, back, back, deep to Russia. To save their life.

INT: So you're referring to the Jewish people, now.

LD: Pardon me?

INT: Are these the Jewish people you're referring to?

LD: I'm talking-- yeah, the only Jewish people.

INT: The ones who left-- did you hear from them? Did you hear where they went? Did you hear what became of them?

LD: No. No. We usually-- even family didn't know. It was so much mixed up. Was lost-- the husband lost the wife, and the wife lost her husband. And some time, the sirens is alarming to-- to run away when the German planes came to bomb the train.

In these kind of panics people lost each the other. They lost the children. Some lost the husband, others the wife. Nobody know what direction--

We-- after the war, we find out. Some of my landsmen was in Siberia, some of my landsmen was in [INAUDIBLE]. We just after the war, we find out.

INT: So when the war broke out, you-- you had German planes coming in and bombing?

LD: Oh, there was bombing every day.

INT: This was something totally new. Was it?

LD: Pardon me?

INT: Was this something totally new, this-- the planes flying in and bombing? Was that something different?

LD: For us, it was new, absolutely. They was flying and bombing, and they-- they was-- the-- the planes prepared the road for the army to come in. They bombed-- they-- they attacked the population. They scared them. And after the-- the planes bombing, the army start to-- to go-- to come in.

INT: So you were there when the German army started to come in?

LD: You see, what-- my parents left. I refused to leave it. I was attached to my grandfather and grandmother. And I was-- I have a big, deep feeling, and I was worried who will take care for them. And I told my parents, I'm going to stay with the grandfather. Everybody left, and I was-- I was left with my grandfather and grandmother.

INT: When did your parents leave?

LD: My parents left in-- June the 22nd, 2:00 in the night, it-- the war start. My parents left in June the 28th, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the town.

INT: 19--?

LD: 1940-- '41.

INT: Where did they go?

LD: I personally didn't know. Nobody know where he's going. They left, and that's all. To run away from the city, and to go with the Russian army. With the Russian army is going to go with them, deep to Russia, just for protection. They didn't know where they can find a protection. People left by instinct. They gone by instinct.

INT: So your parents followed the Russian army.

LD: Yeah-- the Russian army stepped out, and the people stepped out with them. Under their protection.

INT: And who did they take with them, at the time, your-- your parents?

LD: Pardon me?

INT: Who did they take with them, at the time?

