INT: When was that?

LD: This was-- this was in June-- in June 29-- June 30. End of June.

INT: 1941?

LD: '41.

INT: So you were about 10 years old, at the time.

LD: No, 13 and a half.

INT: 13 and a half. Where did she suggest that you go?

LD: I didn't know where to go. I never left home. I took my bicycle. My grandmother gave me a blanket. She gave me a bread. And she gave me a little bit butter, a little bit-- to-- to the bread. And I kissed them goodbye, and I gone by instinct.

I asked people, where's the road to go to Russia? And the people show me the road to go to Russia. And this is the hand which I tell goodbye my parents-- my grandmother and grandfather, and I left.

And I left-- I couldn't go with the highways. Bombed-- they was bombed, the highways. Then some peoples gone-- we-- we have lots of forests over there. Then I was gone in forest.

And then I find out already was surrounded, with the German army, around, which some people find a way to go out. And we was out from this surrounding, from the German army in the forest.

And I was-- I slept. I was tired. And when I woke up in the morning, my bicycle is gone, my blanket is gone, and some food I have which I find in the road-- some potatoes, a couple greens. And everything disappeared, and I left like this-- hungry, tired, my clothes already damaged from the forest, which I was sleeping, over there. And then I didn't have another choice, like, to go together with the army.

INT: So how long were you running in the forest?

LD: I was in the forest-- it was, I would say, eight, nine days, I was sleeping and-- and running in the forest.

INT: What did you use for food, in those days?

LD: You see, in forest, always you can find wild apple. And summertime is not problem, something [? to. ?] You-- I used to go out to our garden, when I used to see our garden. I used to go out at nighttime, something to pick up from garden. Cucumber or something.

INT: Did you come across other people who were running, at the time?

LD: Yeah, no, you see, everybody was of his own. And when people was already-- like, two brothers or two sisters, with their mother, then there was a way-- you-- you didn't know where's the luck direction-- or where is the luck. You never know.

Some people gone to the left, and some people gone this direction. Some people figure out that they can save their life-- they have a little bit of gold or money, and they can save their lives. Some farmers will help them to save their life, which-- they took away the money from them, and they give them over to the German.

No, the feeling what the people have, their instinct, was just false, what the people had.

INT: People went by instinct.

LD: Yes. And this is-- I was alone. I would say five days I was alone in forest.

INT: So did you attach yourself to any groups, during that time-- any groups for support, or to stay with, or--?

LD: No. What I said-- then I was already exhausted. And I came out from forest to the highway. Then I saw it-- army is stepping out-- some is injured-- with wagons. And I was with them together.

INT: Which army was this?

LD: Russian.

INT: Now, at that time is when you assumed your new identity?

LD: No. Pardon me. Not-- after this time, I was with them together. And we came to Korosten with the army. Korosten-- this is a small town in Ukraine, which was a respublik from Russia. And when we came in Korosten, they accept me like a son in the army.

And they have lots of children in the Russian army. Which they used them, sometime, to-- to give them information from the German army. But the German didn't give attention for young kids. And the young kids could go and to sneak out. And it was lots of children, when they lost their parents, was in the army. And I'm one from them.

INT: So you attached yourself to the Russian army for support.

LD: Yes. They give me this army pair of pants, which they didn't have pants for children, and I was small. And big pants, a big shirt-- big boots. When was raining, my boot always left behind. My feet came out from the boot-- very big.

And I was in the army. And then, in the nighttime, they gave me a gun-- which, already, they learn me the way to use a gun. And I was in patrol.

I was in nighttime. I was watching a building over there. And I slept. And somebody stole my gun.

And when they stole my gun, the military tribunal took me-- they took me away. And they put me in a quarantine-- like a jail, in a basement. And then this start already my problem, which I didn't understand the meaning, what is the tribunal. The military tribunal in time from war. And I didn't understand, very serious is, in time from war, to lose a gun. And they put me in basement.

And I think is looking like a Jewish investigator in the military tribunal. And he came in to interview me. And I was crying-- a young kid. And I told him-- they figure up I sold that or I changed for food. And I told him just what happened. I slept, and--

And you're not supposed to sleep when you're a soldier. No, what kind soldier I was? I was a young child. And I told him what happened.

He said, the tribunal don't believe in tears. And he was looking out the window, and he said, I-- I can't tell you what to do. Do what you want.

